1|//! [ratatui](https://github.com/tui-rs-revival/ratatui) is a library used to build rich
2|//! terminal users interfaces and dashboards.
3|//!
4|//! ![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tui-rs-revival/ratatui/master/assets/demo.gif)
5|//!
6|//! # Get started
7|//!
8|//! ## Adding `ratatui` as a dependency
9|//!
10|//! Add the following to your `Cargo.toml`:
11|//! ```toml
12|//! [dependencies]
13|//! crossterm = "0.26"
14|//! ratatui = "0.20"
15|//! ```
16|//!
17|//! The crate is using the `crossterm` backend by default that works on most platforms. But if for
18|//! example you want to use the `termion` backend instead. This can be done by changing your
19|//! dependencies specification to the following:
20|//!
21|//! ```toml
22|//! [dependencies]
23|//! termion = "1.5"
24|//! ratatui = { version = "0.20", default-features = false, features = ['termion'] }
25|//!
26|//! ```
27|//!
28|//! The same logic applies for all other available backends.
29|//!
30|//! ### Features
31|//!
32|//! Widgets which add dependencies are gated behind feature flags to prevent unused transitive
33|//! dependencies. The available features are:
34|//!
35|//! * `widget-calendar` - enables [`widgets::calendar`] and adds a dependency on the [time
36|//! crate](https://crates.io/crates/time).
37|//!
38|//! ## Creating a `Terminal`
39|//!
40|//! Every application using `ratatui` should start by instantiating a `Terminal`. It is a light
41|//! abstraction over available backends that provides basic functionalities such as clearing the
42|//! screen, hiding the cursor, etc.
43|//!
44|//! ```rust,no_run
45|//! use std::io;
46|//! use ratatui::{backend::CrosstermBackend, Terminal};
47|//!
48|//! fn main() -> Result<(), io::Error> {
49|//!     let stdout = io::stdout();
50|//!     let backend = CrosstermBackend::new(stdout);
51|//!     let mut terminal = Terminal::new(backend)?;
52|//!     Ok(())
53|//! }
54|//! ```
55|//!
56|//! If you had previously chosen `termion` as a backend, the terminal can be created in a similar
57|//! way:
58|//!
59|//! ```rust,ignore
60|//! use std::io;
61|//! use ratatui::{backend::TermionBackend, Terminal};
62|//! use termion::raw::IntoRawMode;
63|//!
64|//! fn main() -> Result<(), io::Error> {
65|//!     let stdout = io::stdout().into_raw_mode()?;
66|//!     let backend = TermionBackend::new(stdout);
67|//!     let mut terminal = Terminal::new(backend)?;
68|//!     Ok(())
69|//! }
70|//! ```
71|//!
72|//! You may also refer to the examples to find out how to create a `Terminal` for each available
73|//! backend.
74|//!
75|//! ## Building a User Interface (UI)
76|//!
77|//! Every component of your interface will be implementing the `Widget` trait. The library comes
78|//! with a predefined set of widgets that should meet most of your use cases. You are also free to
79|//! implement your own.
80|//!
81|//! Each widget follows a builder pattern API providing a default configuration along with methods
82|//! to customize them. The widget is then rendered using [`Frame::render_widget`] which takes
83|//! your widget instance and an area to draw to.
84|//!
85|//! The following example renders a block of the size of the terminal:
86|//!
87|//! ```rust,no_run
88|//! use std::{io, thread, time::Duration};
89|//! use ratatui::{
90|//!     backend::CrosstermBackend,
91|//!     widgets::{Block, Borders},
92|//!     Terminal
93|//! };
94|//! use crossterm::{
95|//!     event::{self, DisableMouseCapture, EnableMouseCapture},
96|//!     execute,
97|//!     terminal::{disable_raw_mode, enable_raw_mode, EnterAlternateScreen, LeaveAlternateScreen},
98|//! };
99|//!
100|//! fn main() -> Result<(), io::Error> {
101|//!     // setup terminal
102|//!     enable_raw_mode()?;
103|//!     let mut stdout = io::stdout();
104|//!     execute!(stdout, EnterAlternateScreen, EnableMouseCapture)?;
105|//!     let backend = CrosstermBackend::new(stdout);
106|//!     let mut terminal = Terminal::new(backend)?;
107|//!
108|//!     terminal.draw(|f| {
109|//!         let size = f.size();
110|//!         let block = Block::default()
111|//!             .title("Block")
112|//!             .borders(Borders::ALL);
113|//!         f.render_widget(block, size);
114|//!     })?;
115|//!
116|//!     // Start a thread to discard any input events. Without handling events, the
117|//!     // stdin buffer will fill up, and be read into the shell when the program exits.
118|//!     thread::spawn(|| loop {
119|//!         event::read();
120|//!     });
121|//!
122|//!     thread::sleep(Duration::from_millis(5000));
123|//!
124|//!     // restore terminal
125|//!     disable_raw_mode()?;
126|//!     execute!(
127|//!         terminal.backend_mut(),
128|//!         LeaveAlternateScreen,
129|//!         DisableMouseCapture
130|//!     )?;
131|//!     terminal.show_cursor()?;
132|//!
133|//!     Ok(())
134|//! }
135|//! ```
136|//!
137|//! ## Layout
138|//!
139|//! The library comes with a basic yet useful layout management object called `Layout`. As you may
140|//! see below and in the examples, the library makes heavy use of the builder pattern to provide
141|//! full customization. And `Layout` is no exception:
142|//!
143|//! ```rust,no_run
144|//! use ratatui::{
145|//!     backend::Backend,
146|//!     layout::{Constraint, Direction, Layout},
147|//!     widgets::{Block, Borders},
148|//!     Frame,
149|//! };
150|//! fn ui<B: Backend>(f: &mut Frame<B>) {
151|//!    let chunks = Layout::default()
152|//!         .direction(Direction::Vertical)
153|//!         .margin(1)
154|//!         .constraints(
155|//!             [
156|//!                 Constraint::Percentage(10),
157|//!                 Constraint::Percentage(80),
158|//!                 Constraint::Percentage(10)
159|//!             ].as_ref()
160|//!         )
161|//!         .split(f.size());
162|//!     let block = Block::default()
163|//!          .title("Block")
164|//!          .borders(Borders::ALL);
165|//!     f.render_widget(block, chunks[0]);
166|//!     let block = Block::default()
167|//!          .title("Block 2")
168|//!          .borders(Borders::ALL);
169|//!     f.render_widget(block, chunks[1]);
170|//! }
171|//! ```
172|//!
173|//! This let you describe responsive terminal UI by nesting layouts. You should note that by
174|//! default the computed layout tries to fill the available space completely. So if for any reason
175|//! you might need a blank space somewhere, try to pass an additional constraint and don't use the
176|//! corresponding area.
